**Proposition 11.3 (The three-vertex residual condition).**
Set $w=C_{02}$, $q=C_{12}$ and $z=C_{01}$. The edge $12$ is additional
to the native menu. Define

$$
\ell=\frac{|w|^2}{Z}-(r-X-Y),\qquad
h=X-\frac{|q|^2}{Z}.
\tag{11.3}
$$

The reference residual satisfies

$$
R_{00}=\kappa-\ell,\qquad R_{11}=h-\kappa,\qquad
z=\frac{w\overline q}{Z}+R_{10}.
\tag{11.4}
$$

Consequently

$$
\ell\le\kappa\le h,\qquad
\left|z-\frac{w\overline q}{Z}\right|^2
\le(\kappa-\ell)(h-\kappa).
\tag{11.5}
$$

Together, these conditions are equivalent to positivity of the principal
$\rho$ block on $\{2,0,1\}$, for the declared $Z>0$. They concern this
$3\times3$ block only, not positivity of the whole $5\times5$ matrix.
For a pure state $R=0$ and $z=w\overline q/Z$.

Proof. The entries of $v$ are $v_0=\overline w/\sqrt Z$ and
$v_1=\overline q/\sqrt Z$. Thus
$(vv^\dagger)_{10}=w\overline q/Z$, proving the orientation in (11.4).
The two affine diagonals give the remaining identities. In reference-first
order the principal block is

$$
\begin{pmatrix}
Z&w&q\\
\overline w&r-X-Y+\kappa&\overline z\\
\overline q&z&X-\kappa
\end{pmatrix}.
$$

Its Schur complement is the Hermitian matrix

$$
\begin{pmatrix}
\kappa-\ell&\overline{z-w\overline q/Z}\\
z-w\overline q/Z&h-\kappa
\end{pmatrix}.
$$

Such a two-by-two matrix is PSD exactly when its two diagonals and
determinant are nonnegative, including zero-diagonal cases. This gives
both directions of (11.5). In the full matrix other residual entries
remain unspecified. The normalized version is the reference disk of
[Hidden Relations C-9.2][G11]; the displayed matrices specify its
probability weights and conjugations for the current source. $\square$

